From: PwndaWallet swap sidecar
Date: 2026-08-20
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] basicswap: split the Monero wallet-rpc clients so a host
 wallet can be the main wallet

The XMR interface multiplexes ONE monero-wallet-rpc between the main wallet and
the per-swap 2-of-2 b-lock wallets, serialised behind `_mx_wallet`. That is
sound while the engine owns the process, and unsound the moment anything else
does: monero-wallet-rpc serves one open wallet at a time, so an outside client
issuing `transfer` during the window a swap wallet is open would spend from the
SWAP's wallet, not the user's. Upstream already ships defensive
wrong-wallet-open checks in findTxB and spendBLockTx for the same reason.

So an embedding host that already runs the user's Monero wallet cannot share
this client. It can share a dedicated one. This patch adds a second wallet-rpc
client, selected per operation by WHICH WALLET FILE the operation opens:

  * main wallet  -> `mainwalletrpc{host,port,auth}`, the host's process, which
    only ever holds the user's wallet open;
  * every per-swap wallet -> the existing client, the engine's own process,
    with keys derived per swap and no overlap with the user's key set.

Two processes, disjoint wallet files, and no multiplexing across the boundary —
which is what makes the arrangement safe rather than merely convenient.

The routing rule is upstream's own structure, not a new convention: every
main-wallet operation in this file opens `self._wallet_filename` first, and
every per-swap operation opens a wallet named for the swap's shared address. The
filename is therefore not a proxy for the decision, it IS the decision, so
`_selectWallet` is driven from it and all ~40 call sites keep their exact shape.

Three consequences are handled explicitly rather than left to luck:

  * `openWallet` never issues open_wallet/close_wallet for a host-managed main
    wallet. It confirms the address instead and caches it — the confirmation is
    deliberately not skippable, because it is what turns a misconfigured port
    into a loud failure rather than silent operation on a stranger's wallet.
  * `createWallet` and `changeWalletPassword` refuse to act on a host-managed
    main wallet. Neither is the engine's to do, and the second would lock the
    host out of its own wallet.
  * `testDaemonRPC` and `getDaemonVersion` touch no wallet and would otherwise
    inherit whichever client the previous operation left selected. They pin to
    the main client, which also makes the health check exercise the connection a
    user is most likely to have broken.

With `mainwalletrpcport` absent both names bind to the same client and every
path is byte-for-byte upstream's, so this is a no-op for every existing
deployment and for the WOW interface that subclasses this one.

Not an upstream-PR candidate: it exists to serve an embedding host.

Applies to: basicswap v0.18.7 (079a0d43ed16590eecda2f3d6a3481f847360175)
Marker in patched source: PWNDA-PATCH-5
